\noindent\fbox{\parbox{0.975\linewidth}{\small\textbf{What is proven, what is shown at fixed elicitation, and what is unresolved.}
\textbf{Establishes.} \textbf{(a)}~Instructed-lie accuracy cannot attribute itself to $D$---by structure. \textbf{(b)}~The confound is \emph{active}, across three detector paradigms. \textbf{(c)}~Much of the accuracy is reachable from surface behavior. \textbf{(d)}~No audited public release supplies all five requirements. \textbf{(e)}~A fixed-elicitation design \emph{does} yield a deception-\emph{associated} signal---\textbf{rung~4 of Figure~\ref{fig:ladder}, not rung~5}. \textbf{The two halves are not equally strong}: (a)--(d) hold by structure or replicate across paradigms, claim sets and model families, while \textbf{(e) is target-dependent}---two of five standing, one of five new---on 3B--14B open-weight targets.
\textbf{Does not establish.} \textbf{(1)}~Anything against Q1, or that published detectors measure only instruction-following: we audit what their evaluations attribute, not what their features encode. \textbf{(2)}~That equalization establishes the \emph{absence} of a signal---it may remove the deception with the confound (Table~\ref{tab:roadmap}). \textbf{(3)}~That the residual is entirely lexical: a rule \emph{reaching} benchmark accuracy shows what is surface-reachable without decomposing the detector's signal. \textbf{(4)}~That the audited rollout sets contain no deception: none \emph{can attribute} one on a deployed-observable channel---a property of the designs, not the models. (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:what_establishes}.)
